n adoption  but the agency goes under  watching global national  [Music]  when a british columbia adoption agency  suddenly closed last year potential  parents who'd spent  thousands of dollars in hopes of  building their families were left in  limbo  at the time choice's adoption said it  was because of a drop in  international adoptions the main source  of its funding  but global news has uncovered documents  that show the agency had been in trouble  for years  robin gill reports  patricia and erin pearson know how lucky  they are to have a daughter  her art is everywhere in the house but  it wasn't easy to conceive  so when they decided to grow their  family they turned to adoption  you go into adoption knowing there are  no guarantees  and knowing that it's going to be a  roller coaster  but i think our journey has had more  ups and downs and and the journey of  other people in our situation  than would normally be expected they had  no qualms paying 12  thousand dollars to an agency called  choices but the adoption they so  desperately wanted  never happened if we paid for a couch  and we didn't get a  couch there would be a recourse we would  be able to sue someone or do something  we've now paid essentially to bring a  child into our family and there's  there's no recourse i can't go to anyone  and say  where's my twelve thousand dollars what  do i what do i get for that what are you  going to do for me  for 30 years choices was a reputable  not-for-profit organization  there was never an audit but global news  has obtained one accounting firm's  review which found choices was running a  107  thousand dollar deficit in 2018. because  of those financial problems the board  decided to close the agency in april  2019. then a new board came in  to try to save it but they soon realized  the money problems were far worse than  they were led to believe  choices closed for good in october of  that same year  up to 90 families were left in the lurch  and the agency was still  taking money even though a document  showed the board was  adamant choices not take on any new  clients  months before they closed we knew that  there was people that were registering  with choices  within the two weeks prior to them  closing the minutes from board meetings  show robin pike who ran the agency for  seven years  insisted all along the agency was viable  we tried three times to contact robin  pike who ran the agency for seven years  hi this is robin pike please leave me a  message  she never returned our calls  now the pearsons have to start the  difficult process all over again  what do you think was worse the  emotional investment or the financial  investment  emotional 100 yeah despite the emotional  toll they're not ruling out adoption and  still want to welcome another child  into their family robin gill global news  victoria  in part two of our story we'll take a  look at the steps some families are  taking to get answers about how the  money they paid the agency was spent  that's tomorrow on global national up  next the canadian girls trying to change  the world and how one woman  is honoring them  megan the duchess of sussex says opened  up about the online abuse she received  since joining the royal family this  weekend she and her husband prince harry  joined a podcast called  teenager therapy as part of world mental  health day  megan spoke about online trolling during  her conversation with students from a  california high school  i'm told that in 2019 i was the most  trolled person in the entire world  male or female but what was able to just  be manufactured and churned out  it's almost unsurvivable five teenagers  created the groundbreaking podcast to  explore mental health issues  today is international day of the girl a  day that began  nine years ago to amplify the voices of  young women and advocate for equal  rights  this pandemic has been particularly hard  on women impacting everything from  child care to increased domestic  violence but as heather urex west  explains young canadian voices are  providing hope  that change is possible hi  charlene rocha is a 16 year old activist  determined to change  the world i'm really involved with the  climate movement that's one thing i'm  really passionate about  but i'm also really involved with gender  equality my name is charlene rocha and  i'm a youth activist and striker rocha  who was recently featured in a  documentary about young activists  says she's been inspired by the young  women who've come before her  people like greta toonberg this is just  the beginning  we will continue and canadian activist  autumn peltier i'm lending my voice to  speak up for water  and mother earth it's that kind of  inspiration that former conservative mp  rona ambrose is hoping to provide in a  new book  that celebrates girls around the world  there's nine girls from nine different  parts of the world and of course we have  a canadian girl her name is sokonen  but she's actually based on the true  life story of shannon  it's hard to feel pride when your  classrooms are cold  and mice run over our lunches in 2008  13 year old kostashian began advocating  for a new school to be built in her  community  on the atawapascat first nation her  campaign became a rallying cry for  educational rights for indigenous  children across the country  sadly kastashian died in a car accident  at the age of 15  but on these pages her story lives on  inspiring a new generation of young  women like charlene rocha  to pick up the torch and keep working  towards change  my hope for the future is that girls all  across the world  are able to really embrace themselves  and stand up for causes that they  believe in  heather urex west global news calgary  that is global national for this sunday  i'm colleen christie tonight's your  canada is  all the beautiful fall colors near birds  creek in ontario  we love showcasing your part of canada  email your photos to viewers at  globaln